Why Pip Value Is User-Entered (Not Calculated)
For pairs where neither currency is the account currency (e.g., EURGBP on a USD account), the pip value changes with exchange rates. Calculating this accurately requires a live feed of the conversion rate, which a backtesting tool does not have.

Instead, the user enters the pip value from their broker’s specifications. This is a fixed approximation. It’s not perfectly accurate for cross pairs, but the error is small (typically < 5%) and it avoids enormous complexity.

For pairs where the account currency IS the quote currency (EURUSD on a USD account), pip value is always exactly $10 per standard lot. For JPY pairs on USD accounts, it’s approximately $6.50–$7.50 depending on USDJPY rate.

Step 3: Data Requirement
⚠ All Selected Instruments Must Have Bar Data Loaded
When the session starts, the system must have price data (OHLC bars) loaded for ALL instruments in the session, not just the one currently displayed on the chart.

If the user adds EURUSD, GBPUSD, and USDJPY to the session, all three datasets must be accessible. When the user advances time on one chart, the engine reads the corresponding bars from all three datasets.

If data for a pair is missing or incomplete, warn the user at session creation: “No data loaded for GBPJPY. Please load data or remove from session.”

